---
.title = "Rules",
.description = "Every rule zanity runs, what it flags, and how severe it is.",
.date = .date("2026-10-17T00:00:01"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
---


Rules marked *strict* hold code to what safety-critical software needs and most code doesn't, so they run only with `--strict`, `select = ["strict"]` or `select = ["all"]`, or when named. Rules marked *off* run only with `--rules all`, `select = ["all"]`, or when named.

**NASA's Power of Ten**

| Rule | Severity | Flags |
|---|---|---|
| `recursion` | warning, *strict* | a function that calls itself, directly or through a cycle of calls across files |
| `unbounded-loop` | warning, *strict* | a loop with no bound, such as `while True` or `for {}` |
| `dynamic-allocation` | error, *strict* | allocation after initialization, in Zig and Rust |
| `long-function` | warning | a function with 60 or more lines of code, not counting blank and comment lines |
| `wide-scope` | warning | a local declared in a wider block than the only one that uses it |
| `assertion-density` | error, *strict* | a function with fewer than two assertions that can catch a bug, or a test with no check that can |
| `assertion-message` | warning, *strict* | an assertion with no message |
| `assertion-side-effect` | error | an assertion that assigns or calls something that changes state |
| `forbidden-call` | warning | `eval`, `exec` and other calls that run code no one can review |
| `restated-type`, `constant-assertion`, `redundant-null-check`, `conversion-assertion`, `guaranteed-length` | *strict* | assertions that cannot fail, which do not count towards density |

**Structure**

| Rule | Severity | Flags |
|---|---|---|
| `long-parameter-list` | warning | more than four parameters; not tests, whose parameters are fixtures, or functions whose signature something else fixes, as for `dead-parameter`. Its fix names the parameters to pass as one value: those whose names share a first or last word, and those with defaults |
| `dead-parameter` | warning | a parameter the function's body never uses; not stubs, tests, `_`-prefixed names, methods an interface fixes such as `__exit__`, overrides of a base class's method, decorated or annotated functions, or functions their file passes as a value, such as a callback (not Zig, whose compiler already rejects these) |
| `passthrough-wrapper` | warning | a function whose whole body passes its own parameters, unchanged and in order, to another call; not one that fixes or transforms an argument, a decorated one, or a method delegating to another object |
| `message-chain` | warning | code that reaches through objects, such as `order.customer.address.city`: three member accesses from a local or parameter, or two from a field of `self`; module paths such as `xml.etree.ElementTree` are fine, and so are tests, which configure mocks and check results this way |
| `duplicated-expression` | warning | a computed expression (a call, an index or arithmetic) written three or more times in one function with nothing it reads changing in between; not plain field reads, calls made for their effect, values returned on separate early exits, calls that make a new object or give a new value each time (`Event()`, `time.monotonic()`), types such as `dict[str, Any]`, or tests, which repeat steps on purpose |
| `structural-twins` | warning | functions whose bodies have the same structure, differing only in names and values, across files; tests and functions under six lines are left out |
| `dead-symbol` | warning | a function, method, class or type nothing anywhere refers to; not exported or `pub` API, decorated or annotated code a framework reaches, tests, or entry points such as `main` |
| `single-impl-abstraction` | information | an interface, trait, protocol or abstract class with exactly one implementation (not Go, whose interfaces are implicit) |
| `extractable-block` | information | in a long function, the longest run of statements that reads at most four names from before it, sets at most one read after it, and doesn't return or break out, so it can become a function of its own; with long-function on, it becomes that finding's fix |
| `swallowed-error` | warning | an error handler that does nothing |
| `empty-block` | warning | an empty block where code was expected |
| `deep-nesting` | warning | code nested too deep to follow |
| `complex-function` | warning | a function or test with too many paths through it |
| `long-file` | warning | a file too long to hold in mind |

**Tests**

| Rule | Severity | Flags |
|---|---|---|
| `long-test` | warning | a test with 50 or more lines of code, not counting blank and comment lines |
| `undocumented-test` | warning | a test with 20 or more lines of code and no docstring, comment or sentence for a name saying which behaviour it checks |
| `eager-test` | warning | a test that makes more than 10 checks, counting assertions and test-framework checks such as `expect` and `assertEquals` |
| `sleep-in-test` | warning | a test that waits on the clock |
| `polling-loop` | warning | a loop in a test that polls with a sleep |
| `nondeterministic-test` | warning | randomness or the current time in a test |
| `test-double` | warning | a mock, stub, spy or patch that replaces real behaviour, in a test or anywhere in a test file, such as a fixture or a `@patch` decorator; not a patch of a boundary the language's tables list, such as `requests.get`, `time.monotonic` or `subprocess.run`, nor a module mock of a package, such as `vi.mock("node:child_process")`, which is how a test is meant to isolate itself. A module mock of the project's own code, such as `vi.mock("./db")` or `jest.mock("@/services/x")`, is reported. Its fix names what a patch replaces |
| `call-verification` | warning | a test that checks how a test double was called, such as `assert_called_with`, `toHaveBeenCalledWith` or `verify(mock)`, rather than what the code did |
| `branching-double` | warning | a test double given behaviour that branches on what it is called with, such as `vi.fn((url) => url.includes("/users") ? users : orders)` or a `mockImplementation` that switches on its arguments: the fake re-implements what it replaces |
| `storage-read-in-test` | warning | a test, or a helper in a test file, that reads the database directly to check what the code did, such as Drizzle's or Prisma's `findMany`/`findFirst`, `db.select().from(table)` or a `sql` template that SELECTs, rather than going through the code's own interface |
| `shared-state-in-test` | warning | a test that changes process-wide state: an environment variable, the working directory, the import path, a global default or a `global` |
| `filesystem-in-test` | warning | a test that reads or changes real files outside its own temporary directory |
| `network-in-test` | warning | a test that makes a real network request or connection |
| `database-in-test` | warning | a test that connects to a real database; in-memory databases are fine |
| `unmanaged-temp-in-test` | warning | a test that makes temporary files its framework doesn't clean up |
| `stdin-in-test` | warning | a test that reads what someone types, such as `input()`, `fmt.Scanln` or `prompt`, so it hangs in CI |
| `process-in-test` | warning | a test that starts a real process |
| `vague-test-name` | warning | a test name that doesn't say what behaviour it expects: `test_1`, `it("works")`, or a single word like `test_parse` (Go's `TestParse`, named after the function, is fine) |
| `opaque-check` | warning | a yes/no check of a comparison that fails without the values, such as `self.assertTrue(a == b)`, `expect(a === b).toBe(true)`, `assertTrue(a.equals(b))` or `try std.testing.expect(a == b)`, with the specific check to write instead |
| `broad-expected-error` | warning | a test that expects any error at all: `pytest.raises(Exception)`, `assertThrows(Exception.class, ...)`, a bare `toThrow()`, or `#[should_panic]` without `expected` |
| `skipped-test` | warning | a test turned off with no condition: a skip mark, `t.Skip()` as the test's own statement, `#[ignore]`, `@Disabled`, `it.skip`, or an expected failure that isn't strict |

**Names**

| Rule | Severity | Flags |
|---|---|---|
| `name-drift` | warning | one concept spelled several ways, such as `order_total` and `total_order`; not the same words written by convention, such as a class `FallbackModel` and its instance `fallback_model`, or a private `_now` beside `now`. Its fix names the spelling most definitions use |
| `duplicate-name` | warning | the same name defined more than once in one language and one domain or context, where another file could use either: not methods, nested functions, tests, or names private to their module |
| `forbidden-term` | warning | a name using a word `[vocabulary]` or its domain or context bans |
| `non-canonical-term` | warning | a name using an alias where the project has settled on another word, with the name respelled |
| `misplaced-test` | warning | a test in one domain or context whose subject lives in exactly one other |
| `vocabulary-conflict` | error | a vocabulary that contradicts itself: a word both banned and canonical, an alias mapped two ways, or two domains or contexts with nearly the same name |

**Project files** (build, lint, type-check, test and CI configuration: `pyproject.toml`, `tsconfig.json`, `Cargo.toml`, `.github/workflows/*.yml` and others, checked as text)

| Rule | Severity | Flags |
|---|---|---|
| `relaxed-check` | warning | a setting that lets a check pass when it should fail: `continue-on-error: true`, `\|\| true`, `--exit-zero`, `allow_failure: true`, `strict = false`, `ignore_missing_imports = true`, `"skipLibCheck": true` and the like |
| `weakened-check` | warning | any other setting line that weakens a check; needs `--infer` |
| `unscheduled-analysis` | warning | project files that run no strict static analyser on every change, or daily, with zero warnings allowed; needs `--infer` |

**Error messages**

| Rule | Severity | Flags |
|---|---|---|
| `vague-error` | warning | a message too vague to find the problem |
| `cryptic-error` | warning | a message that is only a code or an internal name |
| `unconstructive-error` | warning | a message that does not say how to fix the problem |
| `misleading-error` | *off* | a message that describes a different failure; needs `--infer` |

**Hazards** (each traces to an entry in the engineering error catalogue in `catalogue/`)

`constant-condition`, `float-equality`, `discarded-comparison`, `unreachable-code`, `generic-catch`, `generic-throw`, `debug-leftover`, `hardcoded-secret`, `return-in-finally`, `identity-comparison`, `precedence-trap`, `switch-fallthrough`, `missing-default`, `no-effect-statement`, `tls-verification-disabled`, `weak-hash`, `unsafe-deserialization`, `shell-command`, `sql-built-from-strings`, `secret-in-log`, `wall-clock-duration` and `unawaited-call`.

`parse-error` reports a file tree-sitter could not fully parse; the other rules still run on what it recovered.


# More rules

What the schema says of the rules described above only by name, or not yet.

| Rule | Severity | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `divide-by-zero` | error, on by default | Use the intended non-zero divisor, or validate a variable divisor before dividing. |
| `assignment-in-condition` | warning, on by default | Assign on the preceding line, or compare here with the language's equality operator. |
| `null-catch` | warning, on by default | Check the value for null before using it, and let unexpected null dereferences fail at their source. |
| `empty-synchronized` | warning, on by default | Move the shared-state operation inside the synchronized block, or remove the empty block. |
| `public-finalizer` | warning, on by default | Remove the public modifier and provide an explicit close method for resources. |
| `direct-thread` | warning, on by default | Submit the task to a bounded, application-managed executor. |
| `unsafe-jni` | warning, on by default | Replace it with a safe Java API, or isolate and audit the native boundary with explicit input validation. |
| `public-static-field` | warning, on by default | Make it private, expose behaviour instead of state, and make constants static final. |
| `public-static-array` | warning, on by default | Keep the array private and return an immutable view or a defensive copy. |
| `missing-super-finalizer` | warning, on by default | Remove finalization in favour of explicit resource ownership, or call super.finalize() in a finally block while legacy finalization remains. |
| `missing-super-clone` | warning, on by default | Start with super.clone(), or replace Cloneable with an explicit copy constructor or factory. |
| `public-clone-method` | warning, on by default | Make clone final, or replace Cloneable with an explicit copy constructor or factory. |
| `thread-run` | error, on by default | Call start(), or submit the task to an application-managed executor. |
| `class-name-comparison` | error, on by default | Compare the Class objects directly, or use instanceof when subclasses are valid. |
| `equals-without-hashcode` | error, on by default | Implement hashCode from the same fields as equals, and test the equality/hash contract together. |
| `hashcode-without-equals` | error, on by default | Implement equals from the same fields as hashCode, and test the equality/hash contract together. |
| `struts-public-field` | warning, on by default | Make the field private and expose it only through the validated bean accessor. |
| `suspicious-comment` | warning, on by default | Resolve the defect or incomplete code the marker identifies, then remove the marker; track longer work in the issue system. |
| `prototype-mutation` | warning, on by default | Use an own data property on an object with a fixed prototype; reject __proto__, constructor and prototype as externally supplied keys. |
| `name-behaviour-mismatch` | warning, on by default | Rename it to say what it does, or change the body to do what the name says. |
| `comment-drift` | warning, on by default | Update the comment to match the code, or fix the code if the comment is right. |
| `query-with-side-effect` | warning, on by default | Rename it to say what it changes, or move the change into a separate function. |
| `partial-failure` | warning, on by default | Wrap the steps in a transaction, or undo the earlier steps when a later one fails. |
| `check-then-act` | warning, on by default | Make the check and the action one atomic operation, for example with a lock or a conditional write. |
| `non-idempotent-retry` | error, on by default | Make the operation idempotent, for example with an idempotency key, or stop retrying it. |
| `unit-mismatch` | error, on by default | Convert them to one unit first, and put the unit in the variable names. |
| `boundary-error` | error, on by default | Check the edge values and add a test for each boundary. |
| `missing-authorisation` | warning, on by default | Check the caller's permission before acting. |
| `mixed-abstraction` | information, on by default | Move the low-level detail into well-named helper functions. |
| `order-dependent-test` | warning, on by default | Give it its own state, or reset the shared state before it runs. |
| `combinatorial-test` | information, on by default | Split it into one test per dimension, or parametrise it. |
| `flaky-test` | error, on by default | Inject a fixed clock, seed or fake, or sort before comparing. |
| `slow-test` | warning, on by default | Replace the wait or service with a fake, and shrink the data. |
| `heavy-setup-test` | information, on by default | Simplify the code's dependencies, or move shared setup into a small builder. |
| `unreadable-test` | information, on by default | Name it after the behaviour, name the magic values, and separate arrange, act and assert. |
| `hollow-test` | error, on by default | Assert on the output or effect of that behaviour. |
| `implementation-coupled-test` | warning, on by default | Assert on public results instead of private state or internal calls. |
| `manual-test` | error, on by default | Replace manual input, printing or setup with assertions and automated fixtures. |
| `unfocused-test` | warning, on by default | Split it up, or assert only on the parts that matter. |
| `self-mocking-test` | warning, on by default | Exercise the real code, and mock only its external dependencies. |
| `trivial-test` | warning, on by default | Test the risky behaviour and edge cases, and make the name match what is verified. |
| `repeated-mechanics` | information, on by default | Write the shared steps once as a builder, helper or table of cases named in the domain's words, so each one says only what differs. |
| `mutable-artifact-reference` | warning, on by default | Pin the artifact by immutable digest or commit, and let an update tool propose reviewed changes. |
| `download-execution` | error, on by default | Download a versioned artifact, verify its published digest or signature, then execute the verified local file. |
| `plaintext-config-secret` | error, on by default | Replace the literal with a reference to an environment variable or secret manager, and rotate any credential already committed. |
| `privileged-container` | error, on by default | Run the container without privileged mode and add back only the specific capability it demonstrably needs. |
| `world-writable-permission` | error, on by default | Grant write access only to the owning user or group; do not make deployed paths world-writable. |
| `public-storage` | error, on by default | Keep object storage private and grant access through authenticated, least-privilege policies or short-lived signed URLs. |
| `directory-listing-enabled` | error, on by default | Disable directory indexes and expose only explicitly routed files. |
| `wildcard-cors-origin` | error, on by default | Allow only the trusted origins that need browser access; do not grant cross-origin access to everyone. |
| `unrestricted-framing` | error, on by default | Restrict frame ancestors to 'self' or an explicit allowlist with Content-Security-Policy. |
| `csrf-protection-disabled` | error, on by default | Enable the framework's CSRF protection and require an unpredictable token on every state-changing browser request. |
| `session-expiry-disabled` | error, on by default | Expire authenticated sessions after a bounded idle and absolute lifetime. |
| `authentication-lockout-disabled` | error, on by default | Throttle repeated authentication attempts and temporarily lock or challenge abusive clients. |
| `weak-password-length` | error, on by default | Require passwords of at least 8 characters, preferably longer, and permit password-manager-generated values. |
| `legacy-tls-version` | error, on by default | Require TLS 1.2 or newer and remove SSL and TLS 1.0/1.1 from the negotiated protocol set. |
| `untrusted-search-path` | error, on by default | Remove the current directory from PATH and invoke security-sensitive tools by an absolute trusted path. |
| `default-credential` | error, on by default | Require a unique credential from a secret manager or first-run provisioning; never ship a reusable default. |
| `default-password` | error, on by default | Require a unique password from a secret manager or first-run provisioning; never ship a reusable default password. |
| `cookie-secure-disabled` | error, on by default | Set the session cookie's Secure attribute and serve it only over HTTPS. |
| `cookie-httponly-disabled` | error, on by default | Set HttpOnly on session and authentication cookies so scripts cannot read them. |
| `cookie-samesite-disabled` | error, on by default | Use SameSite=Lax or Strict for sensitive cookies, with a narrowly justified exception where cross-site use is required. |
| `weak-random` | error, on by default | Use the platform's cryptographically secure random generator for secrets, tokens, nonces and security decisions. |
| `constant-random-seed` | error, on by default | Use an unpredictable operating-system seed for production randomness; keep fixed seeds inside tests or reproducible offline simulations. |
| `nested-regex-quantifier` | error, on by default | Rewrite the repeated group so one input character has only one possible match, or use a linear-time regex engine. |
| `constant-cbc-iv` | error, on by default | Generate a fresh unpredictable IV for every encryption and store it alongside the ciphertext. |
| `constant-aead-nonce` | error, on by default | Generate a unique nonce for every encryption under a key; never embed or reuse one. |
| `constant-password-salt` | error, on by default | Generate a unique random salt for each password and store it with that password's derived hash. |
| `rsa-without-oaep` | error, on by default | Use RSA-OAEP with an explicit modern digest for RSA encryption, or prefer a modern hybrid-encryption construction. |
| `parameter-format-string` | warning, on by default | Use a fixed format string and pass the parameter as a value to format, never as the format itself. |
| `parameter-network-target` | warning, on by default | Parse the target and allow only expected schemes, hosts and ports before making the server-side request. |
| `parameter-redirect-target` | warning, on by default | Redirect only to a fixed local route or a destination selected from an explicit allowlist. |
| `parameter-file-path` | warning, on by default | Resolve the path beneath a fixed trusted root and reject absolute paths, parent traversal, virtual device names and symlink escapes before file access. |
| `parameter-xpath-expression` | warning, on by default | Compile a fixed XPath expression and bind caller-controlled values as variables rather than concatenating or evaluating them as XPath. |
| `parameter-ldap-filter` | warning, on by default | Build LDAP filters with the library's escaping or filter-construction API instead of treating a caller-controlled parameter as filter syntax. |
| `parameter-header-value` | warning, on by default | Reject carriage returns and line feeds before placing caller-controlled data in an HTTP header, or use an API that guarantees that validation. |
| `parameter-template-source` | warning, on by default | Keep template source fixed and pass caller-controlled data only as escaped template values. |
| `parameter-expression-language` | warning, on by default | Use a fixed expression or select from an allowlist; never parse caller-controlled text as expression-language code. |
| `parameter-raw-html` | error, on by default | Keep automatic HTML escaping enabled and pass caller-controlled data as a value, not as pre-approved markup. |
| `missing-integer-radix` | warning, on by default | Pass an explicit radix, usually 10, whenever parsing an integer from text. |
| `parameter-window-opener` | warning, on by default | Open caller-controlled links with noopener (and usually noreferrer), or navigate within the current tab. |
| `parameter-allocation-size` | warning, on by default | Validate and cap the requested size before allocation, then pass only the bounded value to the allocator. |
| `xml-entity-expansion` | error, on by default | Disable DTD loading and external or recursive entity resolution, or use a hardened XML parser. |
| `sensitive-query-string` | error, on by default | Send credentials and secrets in an authenticated header or request body, never in the URL. |
| `cleartext-http` | error, on by default | Use HTTPS and verify the peer; keep plain HTTP only for an explicitly local loopback endpoint. |
| `cleartext-credential-url` | error, on by default | Send credentials only over verified HTTPS, preferably in an authenticated header rather than the URL. |
| `insecure-temp-file` | error, on by default | Create and open the temporary file atomically with the platform's secure temporary-file API. |
| `process-exit` | warning, on by default | Return an error to the application boundary and let the owner decide how to stop or recover. |
| `obsolete-call` | warning, on by default | Replace it with the supported standard-library API documented for the current language version. |
| `explicit-finalizer` | warning, on by default | Release the resource explicitly with close/deinit, or leave finalization to the runtime. |
| `parameter-log-message` | warning, on by default | Use a fixed log message and record caller-controlled data in a structured field after removing line breaks and control characters. |
